{
  "id": "greedy",
  "title": "Greedy",
  "category": "Greedy",
  "level": "se",
  "summary": "A greedy algorithm makes the locally best choice at each step and never revisits it. It is fast and simple, but only correct when you can argue that the greedy choice is always part of some optimal answer.",
  "minutes": 12,
  "idea": "Find the choice rule (sort by end time, take the farthest reach, pair heaviest with lightest), then check it is safe: swapping any optimal answer's choice for the greedy one never makes it worse.",
  "recognise": {
    "signals": [
      {
        "see": "Optimise a count (fewest jumps, most meetings, fewest boats) where items can be ordered by one key.",
        "think": "Sort by that key and scan once. Then ask why the first choice can never hurt: that is the exchange argument."
      },
      {
        "see": "At each step one option clearly dominates: it reaches farther, ends sooner or costs less, without closing off later options.",
        "think": "Take it. The proof idea: any optimal plan can swap its choice for the greedy one and stay at least as good."
      },
      {
        "see": "A DP over choices works but every state picks the same kind of option.",
        "think": "That is a hint that a greedy rule exists and the table can be dropped."
      }
    ],
    "phrasings": [
      "Each index says how far you may jump from it. What is the fewest jumps to the end?",
      "Boats carry at most two people under a weight limit. How few boats are needed?",
      "Schedule as many non-overlapping meetings as possible in one room."
    ]
  },
